\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccc@{}}
\toprule
Task & Model & D\%\textsubscript{int} & D\%\textsubscript{det} & $\Delta$D\% & D / I / N \\
\midrule
freeze & Qwen-7B & 55 & 25 & +30 & 6 / 0 / 14 \\
freeze & Llama-8B & 40 & 20 & +20 & 1 / 0 / 4 \\
\midrule
find-non-living & Qwen-7B & 0 & 0 & 0 & 16 / 4 / 20 \\
find-non-living & Llama-8B & 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 / 1 / 1 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Cross-model death rate comparison. Llama pilot: $n{=}5$ episodes per task; Qwen: from Table~\ref{tab:per-task}. $\Delta$D\%: internal minus deterministic death rate. D/I/N: det-preferred / internal-preferred / no-preference labels.}
\label{tab:cross-model}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:cross-model} summarizes the comparison. This pilot ($N{=}20$) provides preliminary directional evidence only: the sample size precludes statistical claims. Nevertheless, observing the same death asymmetry direction in a different model family (Llama vs.\ Qwen) suggests that the pattern reflects a structural property of internal reasoning in interactive environments rather than a model-specific artifact.

